1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,130
Listen to a talk in a history class.

2
00:00:02,130 --> 00:00:05,800
The Magna Carta, also known as the Great Charter

3
00:00:06,160 --> 00:00:09,260
was signed in 1215 by King John of England.

4
00:00:09,260 --> 00:00:13,662
It was a pivotal document that limited the power of the monarchy

5
00:00:13,662 --> 00:00:16,230
and laid the groundwork for constitutional law.

6
00:00:16,230 --> 00:00:18,995
The Magna Carta introduced several fundamental legal principles

7
00:00:18,995 --> 00:00:23,142
including the idea that the King must govern according to the law

8
00:00:23,142 --> 00:00:27,290
and that all free men have the right to a fair trial.

9
00:00:27,290 --> 00:00:33,707
One of its most famous clauses states that no person shall be deprived of life,

10
00:00:33,707 --> 00:00:37,130
liberty, or property without due process of law.

11
00:00:37,130 --> 00:00:42,040
This principle has become a cornerstone of legal systems around the world.

12
00:00:42,040 --> 00:00:48,107
While the Magna Carta was initially intended to address the grievances of rebellious barons,

13
00:00:48,107 --> 00:00:51,140
its influence has extended far since then.

14
00:00:51,140 --> 00:00:55,794
Over the centuries, it has inspired numerous legal documents

15
00:00:55,794 --> 00:00:58,440
including the United States Constitution

16
00:00:58,440 --> 00:01:02,000
and the Universal Declaration of Human Rights.

17
00:01:02,000 --> 00:01:06,360
Its legacy continues to be felt in discussions about individual rights

18
00:01:06,360 --> 00:01:08,040
and the rule of law.

19
00:01:08,040 --> 00:01:12,170
Let's now talk about some of the specifics of this historic document.
